# Building SafeLink

This document describes the reference build configuration for SafeLink v0.1.

SafeLink v0.1 is deliberately version-locked to the hardware and software combination on which it was built and validated.

The first reproduction should use the documented versions without substitution.

# Architecture

SafeLink v0.1 consists of:

```text
ESP32-P4 ECO2 / prev3
    Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7
    ESP-Hosted host 2.11.6
    + SafeLink P4 source patch
    + SafeLink P4 configuration
              |
              | 4-bit SDIO
              | packet mode
              |
ESP32-C6
    ESP-Hosted slave 2.12.9
    stock source
    + packet-mode configuration
```

There is no SafeLink C6 source-code patch in v0.1.

The source modifications are on the P4 host.

# Reference Versions

## ESP32-P4 Host

```text
Processor:                  ESP32-P4
Silicon:                    ECO2 / prev3
Arduino-ESP32:              3.3.7
ESP-Hosted host:            2.11.6
esp_wifi_remote:            1.3.2
Transport:                  4-bit SDIO
ESP-Hosted mode:            Packet mode
SDIO RX optimisation:       Disabled
SafeLink receive buffering: Fixed preallocated pool
```

Reference build provenance:

```text
esp32-arduino-lib-builder:  8cabf2c
ESP-IDF release/v5.5:       87912cd291
```

## ESP32-C6 Slave

```text
Processor:                  ESP32-C6
ESP-Hosted slave:           2.12.9
ESP-Hosted source changes:  None
Transport:                  4-bit SDIO
ESP-Hosted mode:            Packet mode
```

The C6 used during runtime validation identified itself as ESP-Hosted 2.12.9.

# Repository Files Required

The SafeLink implementation is represented by three important files:

```text
patches/p4/safelink-p4-esp-hosted-2.11.6.patch

config/safelink-esp32p4.fragment

config/safelink-esp32c6.fragment
```

The first file contains the P4 source modifications.

The two configuration fragments record the validated transport configuration for each processor.

# 1. Establish the Stock P4 Baseline

Begin with the official Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 ESP32-P4 environment.

The SafeLink v0.1 host baseline uses:

```text
ESP32-P4 ECO2 / prev3
Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7
ESP-Hosted host 2.11.6
esp_wifi_remote 1.3.2
```

The validated ECO2 Arduino library variant is the `esp32p4_es` target.

Do not substitute libraries generated for a different ESP32-P4 silicon revision.

# 2. Preserve a Stock Control

Before applying SafeLink, retain or reproduce an unmodified ESP32-P4 ECO2 library build.

This provides a control against which the SafeLink build can be compared.

The original SafeLink development process first established that the build environment reproduced the expected Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 ECO2 baseline before any SafeLink changes were introduced.

This is strongly recommended when reproducing SafeLink.

# 3. Obtain ESP-Hosted Host 2.11.6

The P4 SafeLink patch is written specifically against:

```text
esp_hosted 2.11.6
```

Confirm that the source tree is actually ESP-Hosted 2.11.6 before applying the patch.

Do not apply the patch to a newer release simply because the file names appear similar.

# 4. Apply the P4 SafeLink Patch

The host patch is:

```text
patches/p4/safelink-p4-esp-hosted-2.11.6.patch
```

From an ESP-Hosted 2.11.6 source tree, first check that the patch applies cleanly:

```bash
git apply --check /path/to/ESP32-P4-C6-SafeLink/patches/p4/safelink-p4-esp-hosted-2.11.6.patch
```

If the check succeeds:

```bash
git apply /path/to/ESP32-P4-C6-SafeLink/patches/p4/safelink-p4-esp-hosted-2.11.6.patch
```

If `git apply --check` fails, stop.

Do not use a force option to make the v0.1 patch apply to a different source revision.

A failed patch check normally indicates that the source does not match the validated baseline.

# 5. P4 Files Modified by SafeLink

The validated SafeLink v0.1 patch modifies four ESP-Hosted host files:

```text
host/api/src/esp_hosted_api.c

host/drivers/transport/sdio/sdio_drv.c

host/drivers/transport/transport_drv.c

host/port/esp/freertos/src/port_esp_hosted_host_sdio.c
```

These are the host-side source changes that implement the SafeLink behaviour.

# 6. Apply the P4 Reference Configuration

Use:

```text
config/safelink-esp32p4.fragment
```

as the reference for the validated ESP32-P4 transport settings.

Important SafeLink settings include:

```text
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_HOST_INTERFACE=y

CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_OPTIMIZATION_RX_NONE=y

CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_BUS_WIDTH=4

CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_CLOCK_FREQ_KHZ=40000

CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_USE_MEMPOOL=y

CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_TRANSPORT_RESTART_ON_FAILURE=y

CONFIG_SLAVE_IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6=y
```

Streaming or alternative SDIO RX optimisation modes are not part of the SafeLink v0.1 configuration.

# 7. Validated P4 SDIO Wiring

The reference P4 configuration records:

```text
CMD:        GPIO 19
CLK:        GPIO 18
D0:         GPIO 14
D1:         GPIO 15
D2:         GPIO 16
D3:         GPIO 17
C6 RESET:   GPIO 54
SDIO clock: 40000 kHz
Bus width:  4 bit
```

These GPIO values describe the hardware on which SafeLink was validated.

A different board may require different physical pin assignments.

Changing the physical pins does not remove the need to preserve the SafeLink transport invariants.

# 8. Ensure the Patched Component Is Selected

This is an important verification step.

ESP-IDF component precedence can cause an unmodified managed ESP-Hosted component to be selected instead of a local modified copy.

A successful compilation therefore does not prove that the SafeLink source was compiled.

The validated build used the patched ESP-Hosted 2.11.6 source as a local project component override.

Before accepting a build, confirm that CMake selects the SafeLink-modified source files rather than an unmodified managed-component copy.

All four modified source files should come from the intended patched component.

# 9. Build the ESP32-P4 ECO2 Libraries

Build the P4 libraries using the Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 library-builder environment and the ECO2 / `esp32p4_es` target.

The resulting library package should preserve the official ECO2 configuration except for the intentionally declared SafeLink transport-policy differences.

SafeLink should not require application-specific source changes.

# 10. Verify the P4 Build

Do not use compiler success alone as the acceptance test.

Verify at minimum:

```text
ESP32-P4 target                   ECO2 / prev3

Arduino-ESP32                     3.3.7

ESP-Hosted host                   2.11.6

ESP-Hosted packet mode            Required

4-bit SDIO                        Required

SDIO RX optimisation              Disabled

ESP-Hosted mempool                Enabled

SafeLink source selection         Confirmed

All four patched files compiled   Confirmed
```

The validated SafeLink build also contained non-log implementation markers from all four patched source files so that accidental compilation of the stock ESP-Hosted component could be detected.

# 11. Keep the SafeLink Arduino Installation Separate

A separate Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 installation or test environment is recommended for the generated SafeLink P4 libraries.

This makes it easier to:

* preserve the original stock installation
* compare stock and SafeLink behaviour
* recover from build mistakes
* avoid mixing libraries from different targets
* avoid accidentally mixing different Arduino-ESP32 releases

Do not mix SafeLink-generated P4 libraries with libraries from another Arduino-ESP32 version.

# 12. Prepare the ESP32-C6

The C6 side does not require a SafeLink source patch.

Use stock:

```text
ESP-Hosted slave 2.12.9
```

and configure it according to:

```text
config/safelink-esp32c6.fragment
```

The critical SafeLink requirement is packet-mode SDIO operation.

In the validated C6 configuration:

```text
# CONFIG_ESP_SDIO_STREAMING_MODE is not set
```

The C6 also uses:

```text
CONFIG_ESP_SDIO_HOST_INTERFACE=y
CONFIG_ESP_SDIO_HIGH_SPEED=y
CONFIG_ESP_SDIO_TX_Q_SIZE=20
CONFIG_ESP_SDIO_RX_Q_SIZE=20
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_USE_MEMPOOL=y
```

# 13. Validated C6 SDIO Wiring

The C6 reference configuration records:

```text
CMD: GPIO 18
CLK: GPIO 19
D0:  GPIO 20
D1:  GPIO 21
D2:  GPIO 22
D3:  GPIO 23
```

These values describe the validated hardware.

Developers using a different P4/C6 board must configure the pins according to their actual wiring.

# 14. Build the C6 Slave Firmware

Configure the stock ESP-Hosted 2.12.9 C6 slave project with the settings represented by:

```text
config/safelink-esp32c6.fragment
```

The fragment may be incorporated into the project's configuration defaults or reproduced through the ESP-IDF configuration system.

After configuration, verify that SDIO streaming mode is disabled.

Build the C6 firmware using the normal ESP-IDF / ESP-Hosted slave build process.

No SafeLink C6 `.c` or `.h` source files need to be applied.

# 15. Flash the C6

Flash the configured ESP-Hosted 2.12.9 slave firmware to the ESP32-C6 using the normal procedure appropriate to the target board.

Preserve a known-working firmware image before replacing an existing installation.

# 16. First P4/C6 Hardware Test

With both processors prepared:

```text
SafeLink P4
ESP-Hosted host 2.11.6
        |
        | 4-bit SDIO
        | packet mode
        |
ESP32-C6
ESP-Hosted slave 2.12.9
stock source
packet-mode configuration
```

perform a basic transport test before integrating SafeLink into a larger application.

Confirm:

* C6 boots successfully
* SDIO initializes
* P4 identifies the ESP32-C6
* host and slave both report packet mode
* Wi-Fi initializes
* access-point scanning works
* connection to an access point works
* network traffic can pass through the link
* SDIO remains operational during normal network activity

The expected transport relationship is:

```text
slave: packet
host:  packet
```

# 17. SafeLink Runtime Behaviour

The important SafeLink behaviour occurs on the P4 host.

During normal reception:

```text
free SafeLink buffer
        |
        v
consume SDIO packet
        |
        v
deliver packet upward
        |
        v
return buffer to pool
```

When the fixed receive pool has no capacity:

```text
no free SafeLink buffer
        |
        v
do not consume another SDIO packet
        |
        v
allow transport back-pressure
        |
        v
wait for receive capacity
        |
        v
resume
```

This bounded receive behaviour is the central SafeLink invariant.

# 18. Application Integration

SafeLink operates below the application networking layer.

An application should not need SafeLink-specific networking code merely to use the modified transport.

The application used for the original integration validation is private and is not required to reproduce SafeLink.

A recommended validation sequence is:

```text
transport initialization
        |
        v
Wi-Fi scan
        |
        v
Wi-Fi connection
        |
        v
network traffic
        |
        v
sustained / burst traffic
        |
        v
application integration
```

# Reference Build Summary

The SafeLink v0.1 reference configuration is:

```text
Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7
        +
ESP32-P4 ECO2 / prev3
        +
ESP-Hosted host 2.11.6
        +
SafeLink P4 source patch
        +
SafeLink P4 configuration
        |
        | 4-bit SDIO
        | packet mode
        |
ESP32-C6
        +
stock ESP-Hosted slave 2.12.9
        +
SafeLink C6 packet-mode configuration
```

Only the P4 requires SafeLink source modifications.

The C6 requirement is configuration, not a source patch.

# Porting to Other Versions

A newer ESP-Hosted release may have substantially changed transport code.

If the v0.1 P4 patch does not apply cleanly:

```text
DO NOT FORCE THE PATCH
```

Instead:

1. identify the equivalent receive and SDIO transport paths in the new release
2. port the SafeLink behavioural invariants
3. preserve bounded receive capacity
4. preserve back-pressure when receive capacity is exhausted
5. verify packet ownership and buffer return
6. validate configuration
7. build and inspect the resulting binary
8. test the port on physical hardware

A port should not be described as a validated SafeLink configuration until it has completed its own hardware validation.
